  "self_critique": "Overall assessment\n\nThe run found a small apparent improvement, from roughly 0.6018 to 0.603617, but did not establish why it improved or whether the gain is robust. Declaring convergence after only two follow-up failures was premature.\n\nWhat the harness/policy did suboptimally\n\n- Node_001 changed too many things simultaneously: sequence history, temporal/session features, watch-time auxiliary supervision, DeepFM, regularization, and three-seed mean-logit ensembling. This makes the result uninterpretable and confounds architecture gains with ensemble gains.\n- The acceptance logic appears inconsistent. Node_001 claimed an improvement of at least 0.002, while the rounded observed delta is about 0.0018, yet it was accepted. Full-precision matched-seed deltas and the actual acceptance rule should be recorded.\n- Missing learning-curve telemetry was acknowledged but not repaired. Nodes_002 and _003 then relied on speculative diagnoses such as metric mismatch and rare-ID memorization rather than measured evidence.\n- Node_002 was internally inconsistent: it predicted about +0.0018 while predeclaring acceptance only at +0.002.\n- The search pivoted from a large composite directly to unrelated listwise training and SSE. It never performed component ablations on the only successful candidate.\n- Reporting only aggregate primary values obscures seed variance, paired deltas, confidence intervals, training cost, and whether the 0.0001 baseline sigma is credible and computed comparably.\n- \u201cConverged\u201d is too strong after four total nodes, especially when node_003 was only about 0.0001 below the incumbent and therefore likely statistically tied.\n\nWhat I would change in the scaffold\n\n- Require matched-seed, full-precision results with per-seed scores, paired deltas, uncertainty, and an explicit acceptance calculation.\n- Require telemetry artifacts before allowing diagnoses based on overfitting, memorization, or optimization behavior.\n- Enforce one major intervention per node, or require a planned factorial/ablation sequence for composites.\n- Separate single-model improvements from ensembling; compare under equal inference and training budgets.\n- Add a \u201cstatistically tied\u201d outcome rather than forcing accept/reject for differences below noise.\n- Do not permit convergence until the incumbent has been replicated and its key components ablated.\n- Make hypotheses quantitatively consistent with their acceptance thresholds.\n\nWhat the next run should try first\n\nFirst, reproduce node_000 and node_001 on the same five or more seeds and report paired deltas without rounding. Evaluate both a single node_001 model and its mean-logit ensemble separately.\n\nIf the gain survives, immediately decompose node_001. The first architecture experiment should add only the causal 12-author history to the FM baseline, with all other fields, loss, regularization, and evaluation unchanged. Then add temporal/session context, DeepFM interactions, and watch-time auxiliary supervision one at a time. This will determine whether there is a real reusable modeling gain or merely a marginal ensemble/composite effect."
